Chapter 26 — The owl and Stain
Curtis was halfway down the block when the voice caught him.
“Beardy-kun, wait up!”
Hina jogged the last few steps, bag bouncing, still in her work clothes. She fell in beside him, cheeks already warm from the short run and everything else.
“Oh. Hey, Hina.” He glanced over. “What’s a kun? I’ve heard chan and kun a few times.”
She blinked, then smiled. “It’s… an honorific. Kun is usually for guys. A little casual. Friendly. Chan is softer. More for girls or close friends.” She tucked hair behind her ear. “I just use it. Doesn’t have to mean anything big.”
Curtis nodded, filing it. “Beardy-kun. Alright. Still getting used to the extras on names.” He adjusted the jacket. “You heading home?”
“Yeah. You?”
“Same direction for a while.” He started walking again. She matched him. The city night moved around them, streetlights catching the cut on his lip and the easy way she stayed close, while the low, steady pull in his chest remained quiet and the new word settled in like the rest of the things he was still learning.
Curtis kept his hands in his jacket pockets as they walked. “Hey. One of my classmates. Two dudes. One of them calls the other Kacchan. The other one calls everyone losers and extras.”
Hina’s mouth twitched. “Kacchan is a nickname. Usually from when they were kids. Chan at the end makes it cute. If he’s still using it, they’ve known each other a long time.” She glanced at him. “The one calling people losers and extras… that’s just him being rude. Some guys do that when they don’t want to admit they care. Or when they really don’t.”
Curtis huffed a short laugh. “Sounds like Bakugo. The Kacchan one is Midoriya. They grew up together. Bakugo still acts like Midoriya personally offended him by existing.”
“And you?” Hina’s shoulder brushed his. “What do they call you?”
“Beardy. Or extra. Depends on the day.” He shrugged. “Haven’t corrected either.”
She smiled, soft. “Beardy-kun works better. Less explosive.”
They kept walking under the streetlights. The cut on his lip had stopped stinging. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the city moved around them and the small lessons in names continued one block at a time.
Curtis’s thoughts drifted while they walked. Ah. Okay. I thought Deku was gay. Not that there’s anything wrong with that.
His phone buzzed. Unknown number. He opened it. A short video filled the cracked screen—the same man from the café, later, cornering the worker and another girl near the back exit. Hands grabby. Both of them crying, trying to push him off. The clip was only ten seconds. It was enough.
Hina noticed the change in his shoulders. “Something the matter?”
Curtis locked the phone and slipped it away. “It’s nothing.” He looked at her, expression even under the streetlight. “Hey, Hina. Can I hold you?”
She blinked, then stepped in without hesitation. His arm came around her shoulders, careful, steady. She fit against his side, warm, the flush immediate. He kept walking like that, the video already sitting behind his eyes, the low, steady pull in his chest no longer entirely quiet, while the city moved around them and he didn’t explain why he’d asked.
Curtis didn’t explain. He stopped, scooped Hina up in a bridal carry, and jumped. She yelped, arms locking around his neck. He didn’t come down. Air pockets formed under his boots—solid enough to push off—and he ran upward into the open sky above the street.
Blink cut the distance. One flash. Then another. The city dropped away and snapped back in pieces. Wind tore at his jacket. Hina buried her face against his shoulder, heart hammering, but she didn’t tell him to stop. Three more Blinks and the apartment building rose under them. He dropped the last few meters, air pockets cushioning the landing in the side alley, and set her on her feet by the entrance.
“You okay?”
Hina’s hair was windblown. Her cheeks were bright. She was still holding his jacket. “I—yeah. Warn me next time?” A shaky laugh. “That was… a lot.”
“Noted.” He kept a hand on her back until she was steady. The video still sat in his pocket. The low, steady pull in his chest had sharpened, but his voice stayed even. “Let’s get inside.”
They headed for the stairs. The night street remained empty behind them, no one having seen the two figures that had simply stopped being on the sidewalk.
Yui looked up from the couch when the door opened. “Oh, Hina. You’re back sooner than I expected.” She paused, then grinned at the windblown hair and the flush that hadn’t faded. “Hey, wait a minute.”
Hina kicked her shoes off, still catching her breath. “What?”
“Did you and Curtis on the way back?” Yui’s grin turned shameless. “Because you look like you just got thoroughly—”
“No.” Hina’s face went redder. “I wish. No. He flew us home.”
Yui blinked. “Flew.”
“Bridal carry. Jumped. Then just… blinked through the air. Air pockets under his feet. We were on the sidewalk and then we were here.” Hina touched her hair, still messy. “It was a lot. He’s downstairs now. Something on his phone. He got quiet.”
Yui’s grin softened into something more curious. “Flew you home and then went quiet. Cryptid boyfriend things.” She patted the couch. “Come sit. Tell me if the flying was hot or terrifying. I need details.”
Hina dropped beside her, the blush lingering, while the apartment settled around them and the low echo of Free Love stayed warm under both their skins. Downstairs, Curtis’s suite light was already on.
Curtis changed fast. The work uniform hit the floor. The black coat, cargo pants, and helmet went on in under a minute. Red slits lit as the seal locked. He was already moving for the door when the earpiece connected.
“Jeeves. Is that café open? Check the street cameras.”
“It is, sir. Police just left. Two officers. They took statements and departed four minutes ago. The individual from the video is no longer on site. Last camera catch shows him heading east on foot.”
Curtis didn’t answer. He locked the suite and hit the alley at a run. Blink cut the first block. Then the next. Coat snapping behind him, boots barely touching pavement between flashes. The city blurred. Streetlights streaked. He didn’t slow until the café’s corner came into view, police tape already gone, lights still on inside, and the direction Jeeves had marked waiting empty ahead of him.
The low, steady pull in his chest had gone sharp. He kept moving.
“Jeeves. Activate the voice changer.”
The filter kicked in. When Curtis spoke again the voice was lower, smoothed, unrecognizable. He stepped through the café door. The lights were half-dimmed. The same worker from earlier and her friend were wiping tables, both of them still shaken. They looked up fast.
“We’re closed.”
Curtis stopped just inside, red slits level, coat still. “I heard you had a problem.”
The two women froze. The one he’d spoken to that morning stared at the helmet, then at the height, recognition and fear mixing. Her friend gripped the rag tighter. Neither of them moved toward the phone.
Curtis didn’t advance. “The man from the video. He’s gone. Police left. I’m not them.” The changed voice stayed even. “Tell me which way he went after he finished with you. I’ll handle the rest.”
The café was quiet except for the low hum of the machines. The worker’s eyes were still wet. She glanced at her friend, then back at the figure in the doorway, and didn’t tell him to leave.
Fear and Free Love hit them at the same time. The helmet and the changed voice should have kept it clinical. They didn’t. Both women flushed under the scare, eyes tracking the red slits, the want mixing with the leftover shake until their voices came out softer than they meant.
“East,” the worker said. “Toward the station underpass. He was laughing. Said he’d be back tomorrow.”
Her friend nodded, gripping the rag. “Black jacket. Scar on his knuckles.”
Curtis set an envelope on the nearest table. Cash. Enough for the night and then some. “Be safe. Treat yourselves tonight.”
He didn’t wait for thanks. One step back and Blink took him. The door swung on empty air. The envelope stayed. The two women stood in the half-lit café, hearts still racing for more than one reason, while the figure who had asked for the direction was already gone into the streets outside.
The worker’s hands were still shaking. She stared at the helmet, mouth half-open, and couldn’t make the “we’re closed” stick a second time. Her friend had gone pale and pink at the same time, eyes darting between the red slits and the door like she couldn’t decide whether to run or keep looking.
“East,” the worker got out. Her voice cracked. “Underpass by the station. He—he laughed. Said he’d come back tomorrow if we didn’t…” She stopped, swallowed, cheeks hot for reasons that didn’t match the fear still sitting in her chest. “Black jacket. Scar across his knuckles. That’s all I saw.”
Her friend nodded too fast, rag twisted tight in both hands. “Yeah. That. Just—please don’t…” She didn’t finish.
Curtis set the envelope on the table. The bills were visible at the edge. “Be safe. Treat yourselves tonight.”
He stepped back. Blink took him before either of them could answer. The door swung shut on nothing. The envelope stayed. The two women stood there, breathing uneven, one of them pressing a hand to her sternum like she could slow her heart down, the other still staring at the empty doorway with her face burning and her stomach in knots. Neither of them moved to lock the door right away. The want and the scare sat on top of each other, messy and unwanted, while the café hummed and the money waited on the table.
The underpass was mostly empty. One bench under a flickering light. The man sat on it, black jacket open, laughing to himself while he scrolled his phone. A fresh police citation was crumpled beside him. He nudged it with his shoe like it was a joke.
“Third time,” he muttered, grinning. “They always let me walk. Bitches cry, cops shrug, I go home. Easy.”
Curtis watched from the shadow of the support pillar. Helmet sealed. Red slits dimmed. Coat still. The man hadn’t noticed him. He kept laughing, replaying some part of the night on his screen, one hand resting on the scarred knuckles like they were a trophy.
The train announcement echoed down the tunnel. Two minutes. The man pocketed the phone and stretched, still smiling, still certain the night had ended the way it always did. Curtis didn’t move yet. He just watched, the low, steady pull in his chest sharp and quiet, while the citation fluttered on the bench and the man who had made two women cry waited for a train he thought he was still allowed to catch.
Jeeves’s voice cut in, urgent. “Sir, this man is involved in an ongoing sexual assault case involving chi—”
Curtis didn’t let him finish.
Blink. He was in front of the bench before the man could look up. Black smoke erupted from the coat, thick and fast, wrapping the man’s arms and throat and slamming him back against the concrete. The phone clattered. The citation blew off the bench. The man thrashed once, eyes wide, the laugh gone.
Curtis’s hand locked over his mouth. The red slits stared down. No speech. The smoke tightened until the struggling stopped. He hauled the man upright, already turning toward the darker end of the underpass, away from the cameras and the arriving train.
The announcement echoed again. The platform stayed empty. Curtis disappeared into the service tunnel with the man in tow, the low, steady pull in his chest no longer quiet, while Jeeves fell silent in his ear and the citation lay forgotten on the ground.
The service tunnel was narrow and dark. Curtis dropped the man against the wall. Black smoke pinned his shoulders. Before the man could beg, Curtis took both wrists and snapped them. Clean. The cracks echoed. The scream that followed was raw and short—Curtis’s hand covered his mouth again until it died into panicked breathing.
He leaned in. The red slits were the only light on the man’s face.
“Tell me why I should let a vermin like you walk away.”
The man shook. Tears and snot. “I—I didn’t—police already—”
“Police let you walk.” Curtis’s voice was flat through the changer. “Three times. You laughed about it. You made those girls cry and told them you’d be back tomorrow. So talk. Why do you get to keep breathing?”
“It was just—just fun, they overreacted, I never—” The words tumbled. “I have money, I can pay, please, my wrists—”
“Money.” Curtis tilted his head. “You think this is a transaction. You put your hands on people who told you no. You kept doing it because nothing stopped you. Now something has. Give me a reason that isn’t ‘please’ or ‘money.’”
The man sobbed. “I won’t do it again. I’ll leave the city. I’ll— I’ll turn myself in for real this time, just don’t—”
“You had chances to stop.” Curtis’s grip on the broken wrists tightened just enough to make the point. “You used every one of them to do it again. The girls at the café. The ones in the case Jeeves found. All of them. You walked because the system shrugged.” He was quiet for a second. “I’m not the system.”
The man’s voice cracked into something smaller. “Please. I’ll disappear. You’ll never see me. I swear.”
Curtis studied him. The smoke stayed tight. The tunnel was silent except for the wet breathing and the distant rumble of the train that had already left.
“Disappearing isn’t the same as stopping,” he said. “And you’ve already proven you don’t stop.”
He didn’t move yet. He just waited, red slits steady, while the man kept begging and the low, steady pull in his chest remained sharp and cold.
Curtis leaned in closer. The red slits filled the man’s vision.
“And what about the innocence you took from the kids you hurt.”
The man went pale. All the color left his face at once. The begging died in his throat. He knew. The broken wrists stopped mattering. He was dead.
“Ho-how do you know about that,” he whispered. “I—I was proven innocent.”
Curtis shook his head once. “No. No, you weren’t. The case is ongoing. You just managed to have it hidden. Money. A lawyer. A detective who looked the other way. The girls at the café were just the ones who still had the nerve to cry where someone could see.” His voice stayed flat. “The others didn’t get that far.”
The man’s mouth worked. Nothing useful came out. Just a thin, cracked sound. The smoke around his shoulders tightened. Curtis didn’t blink.
“You walked every time because the paper said you could. I’m not paper.”
He straightened. The tunnel was quiet except for the man’s shallow breathing and the distant city. The low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest had gone very still. The decision was already made. He just hadn’t moved yet.
Curtis walked away from the tunnel.
The man was ripped apart. What was left of him had been pinned to the concrete in pieces, black smoke already fading. Blood ran in thin lines toward the drain. Curtis stopped at the wall beside the body, pulled a marker from the coat, and wrote in steady block letters:
THE OWL WATCHES EVERYTHING
He capped the marker. Looked at the words once. Then turned and kept walking, boots even, coat shifting. The red slits dimmed as he reached the tunnel mouth.
“Justice executed,” he muttered to himself.
The city noise grew louder ahead. Sirens were still distant. He stepped back into the night, helmet sealed, and disappeared between the streets while the message dried on the wall behind him and the low, steady pull in his chest slowly settled quiet again.
Curtis had made it three blocks when the scarf dropped from the rooftop ahead of him.
Stain landed in the alley mouth, blades already drawn, eyes sharp under the wraps. Blood still marked the split on his brow from their last meeting. He didn’t waste time.
“I saw the tunnel.” His voice was low, intent. “The man. The message. You execute and you sign it. No speech. No cameras. Just the body and the words.” He took one step closer. “You told me you kill villains. That one was vermin. I do not disagree with the result. I disagree with the silence. A purge without conviction is only murder. So tell me, unknown hero—what conviction lets you leave children avenged and then walk away like the work is finished?”
The red slits faced him. Curtis didn’t reach for a weapon. The alley stayed empty. Distant sirens still hadn’t turned their way.
Stain’s blades stayed low but ready. “Answer. Or I will decide your justice is as false as the heroes I cut down.”
The night held between them. The low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest had gone still again while the Hero Killer waited for a reason that matched the blood.
Curtis’s voice came even through the changer.
“Sexual assault on children makes anyone a villain. Do you not think that way?”
Stain’s eyes narrowed. The blades didn’t drop, but the usual fire banked for a second. He had killed for lesser corruptions of the hero title. This was different. Cleaner. Uglier.
“I think a man who preys on children has already forfeited the right to breathe,” he said, rough. “That is not in question. What is in question is you. You remove him. You leave a message. You do not stay to face what follows. You do not declare why the streets should accept your judgment over the law that failed.” He stepped closer. “Conviction is not only the kill. It is standing in the open with the reason. You hide your face and call it justice. So I ask again—why should anyone trust the owl who watches and then vanishes?”
The alley was quiet. Sirens still distant. Curtis didn’t move. The red slits stayed level on the Hero Killer while the question hung between them, sharper than the blades.
Curtis didn’t flinch.
“Why should I trust someone who also appointed themselves judge and executioner on false heroes, hmm?” The changed voice stayed flat. “Answer my question first. The one I asked the other night. Who died and made us the ones who decide who lives?”
Stain’s jaw tightened. The blades angled a fraction higher. The alley felt smaller. He had demanded conviction. He was being handed the same demand with the mask turned around.
“No one,” he said, low and hard. “That is the point. The system failed. The Symbol refuses to finish what he starts. Someone must stain their hands so the title means sacrifice again. I accepted that burden. I do not hide from it.” His eyes burned. “You hide. You kill and you leave riddles. So do not ask me to justify my blade until you justify the owl. Answer, or we end this the way the tunnel ended.”
They stood three meters apart. Sirens still hadn’t found them. The question from the first night sat between them again, unanswered on both sides, while the low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest remained still and Stain waited to see which of them would blink.
Curtis took one step forward. The red slits didn’t waver.
“Then why do you hide during the day? Why do you wear that mask? Why do you not confront All Might himself and declare what you’re doing?” The voice stayed even. “You talk about standing in the open. You kill at night. You cover your face. You leave the Symbol alive and call it respect. If conviction means facing the consequence, go tell him to his face that his mercy is the reason your list exists. Or admit we’re the same. Judges who picked the dark because the light asks questions we don’t want to answer.”
Stain’s breath came harsh. The blades trembled—not from fear. From the hit landing clean. He had demanded the owl justify itself. The owl had just used his own rules against him.
The alley stayed silent. Sirens still distant. Stain’s eyes burned behind the wraps, the ideological fire cracking at the edges for the second time in two nights, while Curtis waited, unmoving, for an answer that didn’t taste like the hypocrisy he’d just named.
Curtis’s voice didn’t rise.
“I kill vermin and left a calling card for the other villains. Not the media. It’s not a riddle. The message is clear. I’m watching.”
Stain’s eyes narrowed. The blades stayed up, but the immediate push to strike had stalled. He studied the red slits, the still posture, the lack of performance.
“A warning to the rest of the filth,” he said, low. “Not a sermon for the cameras. You want them afraid before they act again.” He was quiet a second. “That is cleaner than I expected. It does not make you right. It makes you purposeful.”
The alley remained empty. Sirens still hadn’t turned. Stain’s scarf shifted in the night air. He had not lowered the knives. He had not advanced either.
“Then hear this, owl.” His tone sharpened again. “If your watching ever turns on a hero I have judged false, I will find the face under that helmet. And I will not ask questions twice.”
Curtis didn’t move. “Noted. Same goes if your purge ever protects a man like the one in the tunnel.”
They stood in the quiet. Neither stepped back. The low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest stayed still while the Hero Killer weighed the answer he had been given and the night kept its distance around them both.
Curtis tilted his head. “So what would you have done? Do you only kill false heroes?”
He didn’t wait for the answer. The changed voice stayed level as he laid it out.
“Three open harassment cases the police shrugged off. Two café workers he cornered tonight until they cried. And the hidden one—ongoing sexual assault on children. He paid to bury the file. Lawyer. A detective who looked away. He walked every time. Laughed about it. Told those girls he’d be back tomorrow. That’s not a fake hero. That’s a man who already proved he’ll keep taking from people who can’t fight him. The system had him and let him go. So I finished it. He doesn’t get another night. That’s why he died.”
Stain was silent. The blades had lowered a fraction. His eyes stayed sharp, but the usual sermon didn’t come immediately. He had spent years cutting down heroes who chased comfort. This was a predator the heroes had failed to stop.
“I do not only kill false heroes,” he said at last, rough. “I kill what corrupts. A man who preys on children has already chosen to be removed. The law’s failure does not grant him mercy.” He met the red slits. “You were correct to end him. The method is still yours to justify. But the result… I will not call that one murder.”
The alley stayed quiet. Sirens remained distant. Curtis didn’t relax. Stain didn’t sheath the knives. The agreement sat between them, thin and unwanted, while the night continued and neither of them walked away yet.
Curtis reached up and unsealed the helmet. He pulled it free. Heterochromia met Stain’s eyes in the alley dark—red and blue, steady. The cut on his lip was still there. Braids shifted.
“Call it anger, then.”
He didn’t put the helmet back on. “My name’s Curtis. Curtis Beardy. I’m not hiding from you. You’re too… similar.”
Stain stared. The blades stayed in his hands, but the killing tension had shifted into something sharper and more complicated. He had the face now. The name. The student who had headbutted him, copied nothing he could see, and just executed a child predator without a speech.
“Beardy.” The name sat rough in his mouth. “You give me this and expect what? That I sheathe my knives because we both remove what the system will not?”
“I expect you to know who you’re talking to the next time we meet.” Curtis’s voice was his own again, low and even. “Same as I know you. We can keep pretending the masks make us different. Or we can stop.”
The alley was silent. Sirens still distant. Stain looked at the unmasked face, the helmet hanging at Curtis’s side, and did not strike. The agreement from a moment ago had just gained a name, and neither of them had decided yet what to do with it.
Stain was quiet for a long moment. The blades lowered another fraction. He studied the unmasked face—the heterochromia, the fresh cut, the lack of fear—and something in his posture shifted. Not trust. Recognition.
“Chizome Akaguro.” The name came low, deliberate. “That is what they called me before the title. Before the mask. You have given me yours. I will not remain only Stain to you.”
He didn’t sheathe the knives. He didn’t offer a hand. He simply stood in the alley with the wraps still covering the rest of his face and the real name now spoken between them.
“Remember it, Beardy. The next time we meet, we will both know exactly who is judging.”
Curtis nodded once. He didn’t put the helmet back on yet. The two of them remained where they were, names exchanged, the night still holding its distance, while the low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest stayed still and the sirens finally began to turn in a direction that was no longer theirs.
